{"name":"Tunic Green","hex":"#00cc00","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tunic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A garment worn over the torso, with or without sleeves, and of various lengths reaching from the hips to the ankles.","Any covering, such as seed coat or the organ that covers a membrane."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tunic"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tunic","blocks":[{"text":"A tunic is a garment for the torso, usually simple in style, reaching from the shoulders to a length somewhere between the hips and the ankles. It might have arm-sleeves, either short or full-length. Most forms have no fastenings. The name derives from the Latin tunica, the basic garment worn by both men and women in ancient Rome, and medieval Egypt, which in turn was based on earlier Greek garments that covered wearers' waists."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Coptic_-_Tunic_-_Walters_83484.jpg/330px-Coptic_-_Tunic_-_Walters_83484.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tunic","wikidata":"Q201714"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f5/Tunikakl%C3%A4dd_romersk_arbetare%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f5/Tunikakl%C3%A4dd_romersk_arbetare%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Roman worker dressed in a tunic","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATunikakl%C3%A4dd_romersk_arbetare%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Thorsberg_Tunic.jpg/500px-Thorsberg_Tunic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Thorsberg_Tunic.jpg/1280px-Thorsberg_Tunic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Thorsberg_Tunic.jpg/500px-Thorsberg_Tunic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Germanic tunic of the 4th century AD found in the Thorsberg moor","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AT